{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on an unstated inductive assumption that the 'asymmetry of risk' itself is a reliable guide for future action. By arguing that we should prefer the well-corroborated theory because it has 'survived risks that eliminated its competitors,' the target implicitly assumes that a theory's past survival is a valid indicator of its future reliability or lower risk of falsification. This smuggles in the principle of the uniformity of nature (that the future will resemble the past), which is the very inductive premise Popper sought to reject. If the future does not resemble the past, the fact that a theory survived previous tests offers no rational basis for expecting it to survive future ones, rendering the 'pragmatic wager' indefensible without induction.","premise":"Preferring a theory based on its past survival of severe tests requires the inductive assumption that past performance predicts future reliability.","premises_essential":["a838263ffdb41ffc3317a17d87d039deb272c7a9a7e70b398c9ad492626f0587"],"successor_question":"Can Popper formulate a rule for action that relies solely on the logical status of theories (falsified vs. not-yet-falsified) without implicitly assigning any weight to their history of testing?","target":"a838263ffdb41ffc3317a17d87d039deb272c7a9a7e70b398c9ad492626f0587"}]}